                <pb facs="0497" n="387" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg387"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/2506: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-11-06T17:00:00+01:00">November 6, 1931—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 6—1:05 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>284. Sze has sent a note dated November 4 of about 1,000 words to Drummond
                    commenting on the Japanese Government[’s] reply to the identic telegrams sent
                    from Geneva on October 17th by signatories of the Pact of Paris. Inasmuch as
                    that note seems to contain no new elements but is a mere restatement of the
                    Chinese on the points raised, the text will not be sent by telegraph but is
                    being forwarded by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
